Follow-up to aiecompiler_physical_device_missing.log, which closed AMD's proprietary
aiecompiler path (vaie_cpplus / aiecompiler_client.dll) for --target=hw: physical_device.dll
and three sibling DLLs are absent from every AMD distribution channel checked on this
machine (pip, both offline installers at 1.7.1 and 1.8.0). That log's conclusion offered
two remaining avenues, both "out of scope for this pass": a full Vitis/Vivado install, or
a direct request to AMD. This log is the result of chasing the first of those -- and
finding a materially different, unblocked path instead of the gated one originally implied.

--- What "full Vitis/Vivado" turned out to actually mean ---

The physical place-and-route backend AMD ships standalone is called "Vitis AIE Essentials",
gated behind the "Ryzen AI Software Early Access Lounge" (account.amd.com/en/member/
ryzenai-sw-ea.html: AMD account + approval, then a MAC-address-locked license from
xilinx.com/getlicense). Every reference to it on Windows -- including AMD's own official
guide -- runs it under WSL, not natively; it is also frozen at SDK version 1.3.1 in every
source found, two versions behind the 1.7.1 pipeline already probed. This is the gated,
proprietary route and was not pursued further (WSL cannot reach XDNA1 hardware per this
project's own findings, and the version gap to 1.7.1 is unverified).

Instead: AMD's own current docs say the Vitis installation step can be skipped entirely
for AIE2 (Phoenix/Hawk Point, this hardware) and AIE2P (Strix) by using Peano, the
open-source LLVM-based AIE backend bundled with Xilinx/mlir-aie on GitHub. That project
implements its own place-and-route in MLIR passes and generates the CDO/PDI/xclbin
directly -- it does not call AMD's proprietary aiecompiler or physical_device.dll at all.
No AMD account, no license, no WSL.

Peano was, in fact, already sitting on this machine: `llvm_aie_lightweight-1.7.1` is a pip
dependency of the existing ryzen-ai-1.7.1 env, installed at
Lib/site-packages/win64.o/tools/peano/ (clang.exe, ld.lld.exe, native Windows binaries,
targets aie2-none-unknown-elf and aie2p-none-unknown-elf) -- a completely separate
`win64.o` namespace from `Lib/site-packages/lib/win64.o/` where physical_device.dll was
expected. Two distinct toolchains coexist in the same pip env; only one of them is
missing its backend.

--- Setup (native Windows, no WSL) ---

New conda env (Python 3.13, required for the Windows XRT SDK's pyxrt bindings; none of
the project's existing 5 envs touched):
  conda create -n mlir-aie-iron python=3.13

Cloned to C:\Users\<user>\mlir-aie (outside this repo -- a separate toolchain, not part of
the tracked pipelines), checked out tag v1.4.2 (commit 760932a4abf):
  git clone --recurse-submodules https://github.com/Xilinx/mlir-aie.git
  git checkout v1.4.2

Checked the one real compatibility risk first: mlir-aie's native Windows guide requires
XRT 2.21.0 / NPU driver 32.0.20101.3760. `xrt-smi examine` on this machine already
reported exactly that (XRT 2.21.0, NPU Driver Version 32.0.20101.3760, NPU Firmware
1.5.5.391, device "NPU Phoenix") -- no driver change needed.

`python utils\iron_setup.py` builds a local .\ironenv venv and pulls pinned wheels. Two
snags, both minor and fixed without touching the existing pip envs or system state:
  1. "llvm-objcopy.exe is required to prepare the llvm-aie wheel" -- the setup script
     looks for it under peano_root/bin or PATH and finds neither; the mlir_aie wheel
     (a different package from llvm-aie) bundles its own copy at
     ironenv/Lib/site-packages/mlir_aie/bin/llvm-objcopy.exe. Fixed by adding that
     directory to PATH before rerunning setup.
  2. "No Windows XRT SDK root was found" -- iron_setup.py's own XRT (driver+runtime,
     already installed) is not the same thing as the XRT *SDK* (headers + pyxrt.pyd) it
     needs to build/JIT host code. Downloaded xrt_windows_sdk.zip from the matching
     release (github.com/Xilinx/XRT/releases/download/2.21.75/xrt_windows_sdk.zip,
     70,834,080 bytes, matches the GitHub-reported size), extracted to
     C:\Xilinx\XRT\xrt_sdk\xrt, reran with --xrt-root pointing at it.
Second rerun: "IRON environment is ready." (Python 3.13.15 | mlir_aie 1.3.4 | llvm-aie
22.0.0.2026090201+a36c62b9 | XRT: C:\Xilinx\XRT\xrt_sdk\xrt)

--- First run: version-skew failure, then the fix ---

programming_examples/getting_started/01_SAXPY/saxpy.py against the freshly-installed
mlir_aie 1.3.4 (installed setup.py's rolling "latest-wheels-4" channel) failed immediately
in kernel generation:
  TypeError: Runtime.__init__() takes from 1 to 2 positional arguments but 3 were given
This is the exact version-skew the project's own README warns about: the checked-out
`main` branch's example code had drifted from wheel 1.3.4's API. Fixed by syncing both to
the same tagged release instead of a rolling channel + branch tip:
  git checkout v1.4.2
  pip install --upgrade mlir_aie -f https://github.com/Xilinx/mlir-aie/releases/expanded_assets/v1.4.2
(installed mlir_aie-1.4.2, replacing 1.3.4)

--- Result ---

Cleared ~/.npu/cache and reran saxpy.py from a cold cache to get a genuine compile, not a
cache hit. saxpy.py builds a 2-input, 1-output IRON kernel (SAXPY: z = 3*x + y, N=4096,
bfloat16, one AIE core), JIT-compiles it via @iron.jit, dispatches it against
device="npu" (not a simulator device string), and checks the NPU's output against a numpy
reference with a one-line tolerance assertion. Full captured stdout:

  PASS!

The fresh-compile cache directory (~/.npu/cache/f6e83e5ef3060b585fcb4256/) is the evidence
this was a real compile, not a stale artifact: aie.mlir and input_with_addresses.mlir (the
placed MLIR module), three staged LLVM-IR dumps for the core (peano-compat / opted /
peano-linked *.ll), the compiled kernel object (objects_main_core_0_2.o, from saxpy.cc via
the ExternalFunction mechanism), the linked core ELF (elfs_main_core_0_2.elf), the three
CDO binaries (cdo_main/main_aie_cdo_{elfs,enable,init}.bin -- Configuration Data Objects,
what actually programs the AIE array), main.pdi (Programmable Device Image), and
final.xclbin (8,870 bytes) -- the artifact XRT loads. Every stage of place-and-route ran
through mlir-aie's own tools; aiecompiler_client.dll and physical_device.dll are not in
this path at all.

--- Conclusion ---

The custom-kernel bring-up wall in aiecompiler_physical_device_missing.log is specific to
AMD's proprietary vaie_cpplus/aiecompiler toolchain, not to this hardware or to "writing
custom AIE kernels" in general. A hand-written kernel was written, compiled for
--target=hw, and verified correct against a numpy reference on this machine's actual
XDNA1 (Phoenix) NPU, entirely through the open-source mlir-aie + Peano toolchain, natively
on Windows, with no AMD account, no license, and no WSL. This does not make the
proprietary-toolchain finding wrong -- physical_device.dll is still genuinely absent from
every AMD distribution channel checked -- but it means custom-kernel bring-up on this
hardware is not actually blocked; it just requires a different toolchain than the one
this project's own pipelines (Quark/VitisAI EP) use for quantized-model inference. The two
toolchains are not interchangeable: mlir-aie/IRON is for hand-written kernels from
scratch; Quark+VitisAI EP is for quantizing and running existing ONNX models. This log
closes the "is custom-kernel bring-up possible on this hardware" question with a measured
yes, via a different door than the one that was walled off.

New machine state, none touching the existing 5 conda envs or the tracked pipelines:
  - conda env mlir-aie-iron (Python 3.13, bootstrap interpreter only)
  - C:\Users\<user>\mlir-aie (git clone, tag v1.4.2, own .\ironenv venv inside it)
  - C:\Xilinx\XRT\xrt_sdk\xrt (XRT Windows SDK 2.21.75, headers + pyxrt only -- the
    driver/runtime XRT this machine already had is untouched)

Machine: Desktop 2 / Phoenix.
